---
layout: base.njk
title: "Wire flow in 3D — Pragmatic Agents Lab"
description: "An interactive 3D model of institutional wire processing: drag straight-through processing from 0% to 99.9% and raise the volume to see why the manual desk stops scaling."
permalink: /lab/wire-flow/
---
<style>{% include "css/lab.css" %}</style>

<div class="wf-wrap">
  <section>
    <span class="kicker">Lab · interactive model</span>
    <h1>Wire flow, from the manual desk to <span class="hl">99.9% straight through</span></h1>
    <p class="sub">Each point of light is a payment. Straight-through payments climb the platform, pass the SWIFT ring and arc to the banks. The rest drop to the operations desk and wait. Drag the sliders, then orbit the scene.</p>

    <div class="wf-stage" id="wf-stage">
      <canvas id="wf-canvas" aria-label="3D scene: payments rise from three source pillars and either climb a layered automation platform, pass a SWIFT ring and arc to four bank towers, or drop to a manual operations desk where they pile up"></canvas>
      <div class="wf-labels" id="wf-labels"></div>
      <div class="wf-views">
        <button data-wf-view="overview">Overview</button>
        <button data-wf-view="platform">Platform</button>
        <button data-wf-view="desk">Desk</button>
        <button data-wf-view="banks">Banks</button>
        <button id="wf-orbit" class="active">Orbit</button>
      </div>
      <span class="wf-hint">Drag to orbit · scroll to zoom · right-drag to pan</span>
    </div>

    <div class="wf-panel">
      <div class="wf-ctl">
        <label for="wf-stp">Straight-through processing <output id="wf-stp-out">99.9%</output></label>
        <input type="range" id="wf-stp" min="0" max="999" value="999" step="1">
      </div>
      <div class="wf-ctl">
        <label for="wf-vol">Payment volume <output id="wf-vol-out">×1</output></label>
        <input type="range" id="wf-vol" min="1" max="18" value="1" step="1">
      </div>
      <div class="wf-buttons">
        <button class="arch-btn active" id="wf-journey">Play the journey: 0% to 99.9%</button>
        <button class="arch-btn" id="wf-play">Pause</button>
        <button class="arch-btn" id="wf-reset">Reset</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="wf-stats">
      <div class="wf-stat"><b id="wf-auto">0</b><span>settled straight through</span></div>
      <div class="wf-stat"><b id="wf-manual">0</b><span>settled by hand</span></div>
      <div class="wf-stat"><b id="wf-queue">0</b><span>waiting at the desk</span></div>
      <div class="wf-stat"><b id="wf-rework">0</b><span>sent back for rework</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="wf-notes">
      <p><strong>What to try.</strong> Set straight-through processing to 0% and volume to ×18. The desk has four people, the heap keeps growing and the queue never clears. Now drag processing back to 99.9% at the same volume: the platform absorbs it and the desk handles only the exceptions.</p>
      <p>That is the argument of the <a href="/work/wire-automation/">wire automation case study</a>: the real programme went from 0% to 99%+ straight-through processing and absorbed roughly 18× volume growth without adding headcount.</p>
      <p class="wf-small">This is an illustrative simulation. Speeds, error rates and queue sizes are chosen to show the mechanism and are not client data.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>

